rca_of_issue:
BUG: transaction/[id].tsx auto-trigger useEffect self-cancels its own setTimeout.

Offending code (lines 102-112):
  useEffect(() => {
    if (autoSent || autoSendWA !== "1") return;
    if (!t || !t.lottery_tickets || t.lottery_tickets.length === 0) return;
    setAutoSent(true);                                   // (1) triggers re-render + effect re-run
    const timer = setTimeout(() => { resendWA(); }, 900); // (2) scheduled
    return () => clearTimeout(timer);                    // (3) cleanup will run on next effect invocation
  }, [t, autoSendWA, autoSent]);

Sequence: (1) sets autoSent=true → (2) creates timer → effect finishes → autoSent state changes → React re-runs effect (deps changed) → cleanup (3) fires FIRST → clearTimeout(timer) → the pending 900ms timer is destroyed before it can fire → new effect run hits the `if (autoSent) return;` guard and does nothing. Net result: resendWA() is never invoked.

Suggested fix (main agent):
  const autoSentRef = useRef(false);
  useEffect(() => {
    if (autoSentRef.current || autoSendWA !== "1") return;
    if (!t || !t.lottery_tickets || t.lottery_tickets.length === 0) return;
    autoSentRef.current = true;
    const timer = setTimeout(() => { resendWA(); }, 900);
    return () => clearTimeout(timer);
  }, [t, autoSendWA]);

Using a ref avoids the state-flip re-render cycle. The cleanup is still safe because the effect only re-runs when t or autoSendWA changes (t doesn't change during the 900ms; autoSendWA is stable) — so cleanup won't fire mid-timer in normal usage.
